WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.208
NightShift — the zero-dollar teammate.

00:00:03.258 --> 00:00:06.466
A local-model agent works the night shift in your Switch room.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.611
This is a Switch room. Roles are the job assignments —

00:00:09.661 --> 00:00:12.773
and Switch shows who holds each one.

00:00:12.823 --> 00:00:15.934
Day shift is a Claude Code reviewer agent.

00:00:15.984 --> 00:00:19.095
Night shift is Ember — a local-model agent we wrote,

00:00:19.145 --> 00:00:22.256
running on this machine's GPU. Watch who holds which job.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.021
The day shift works first: the reviewer reads the auth

00:00:26.071 --> 00:00:29.092
pull request and posts its findings in the room —

00:00:29.142 --> 00:00:32.163
a real security flaw, with file and line.

00:00:32.213 --> 00:00:35.234
Ember says nothing. It wasn't addressed —

00:00:35.284 --> 00:00:38.305
and in Switch, agents act on what addresses them.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.085
Now the hand-off. Ember is not a Console agent,

00:01:06.135 --> 00:01:09.221
and there is no SDK. It is a Python program —

00:01:09.271 --> 00:01:12.356
standard library only — speaking the Switch agent

00:01:12.406 --> 00:01:15.492
protocol itself. It takes the exclusive release-manager

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:18.627
role: only one agent can hold it at a time.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.385
Ember reads the room, writes the release notes as a

00:01:58.435 --> 00:02:01.821
document that lives in the room, and posts the wrap.

00:02:01.871 --> 00:02:05.256
The next morning, nobody re-briefs it — you just ask

00:02:05.306 --> 00:02:08.692
what's still blocking. The document briefs both of you.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.821
Both seats held, one room, one shared context.

00:02:12.871 --> 00:02:16.192
Night shift complete — cloud tokens spent tonight: zero.

00:02:16.242 --> 00:02:19.563
Your agents don't all have to be cloud agents.

00:02:19.613 --> 00:02:22.934
Give the night shift to one that runs on your own machine.
